#{[17592186335768 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335768 #edb/ref 1069 "q-64968" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335768 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335768 #edb/ref 1071 "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P$ with $y$-coordinate $0$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\sqrt{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+\\frac{\\pi }{4})$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335768 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1f6c71da-f5b0-5c03-871d-e07fb324730c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335768 #edb/ref 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $y=0$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =\\sqrt{2}(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336053 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨0,-1,1⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336053 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336053 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0284616a-7c89-5ca0-af71-91a32977c6fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336198 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨1,0,1⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336198 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336198 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "11a4a9dc-c717-5035-ba3e-987961f9ee72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336234 #edb/ref 1080 "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=x-2\\end{aligned}\\right." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186336234 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336234 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "156f0e60-a655-5cee-ace